<p>Marketing in English and Spanish in New Mexico works best when you treat Spanish as part of your brand, not a translation you bolt on at the end. Start with the places customers make quick decisions, like your Google Business Profile, your signs and flyers, and the social posts that bring people through the door. Then expand to your website once you know what your customers respond to.</p>

<p>The other half of the answer is quality. Spanish that reads like it came straight out of a translation app can do more harm than staying in English, because it tells people you didn't really have them in mind. A fluent reviewer who knows your local customers is the single most important part of a bilingual plan. The rest of this guide covers where to start, how to set up each channel and the mistakes we see most often.</p>

<h2>Why bilingual marketing matters in New Mexico</h2>

<p>Spanish has been spoken in New Mexico for centuries, and plenty of families here move between English and Spanish every day. For a lot of local businesses, a bilingual customer base isn't a future market. It's the people already walking in.</p>

<p>If you want to see how many households in your area speak a language other than English at home, the U.S. Census Bureau publishes that figure for the state and for each county in <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/NM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">QuickFacts</a>. Look up the counties you actually serve. Albuquerque, Mora and Las Cruces can look very different, and your plan should match your own customers, not a statewide average.</p>

<p>It's also worth remembering that bilingual doesn't always mean "needs Spanish." Many of your Spanish speaking customers read English just fine. What Spanish does is make people feel welcome and recognized, and that's a brand decision as much as a language one.</p>

<h2>Where to start: the places customers decide fast</h2>

<p>You don't need to translate everything on day one. Start with the touchpoints where a few words make the biggest difference:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Your Google Business Profile.</strong> Mention in your description that you serve customers in English and Spanish (if you truly do), and post updates in both languages. Our guide to <a href="/blog/get-business-on-google-maps-albuquerque">getting your business on Google Maps</a> covers the rest of your profile.</li>
  <li><strong>Signs, menus and flyers.</strong> Hours, prices, "open," "now hiring" and "we speak Spanish" are simple phrases with a big payoff. Keep the layout balanced so neither language looks like an afterthought.</li>
  <li><strong>Event and promotion posts.</strong> A festival, a sale or a grand opening is shared widely, so it's a natural place to reach more people in both languages. Our tips on <a href="/blog/event-poster-flyer-design-tips">event posters and flyers</a> apply in either language.</li>
  <li><strong>Your contact options.</strong> If someone on your team speaks Spanish, say so near your phone number and contact form. That one line can be the reason someone calls.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to handle Spanish on social media</h2>

<p>There are two common setups, and both can work:</p>

<h3>Both languages in one post</h3>

<p>This works well for short posts: a photo of today's special, an "open late tonight" update or a holiday greeting. Put one language first, add a line break, then the other. Keep it short, because long posts in two languages get cut off behind "see more" and nobody reads the second half.</p>

<h3>Separate posts for each language</h3>

<p>This works better for longer stories, how-tos and anything with details people need to follow. It also lets you adjust the message, not just the words, for each audience.</p>

<p>Whichever you choose, be consistent. If your followers learn that every post has both languages, they'll look for it. Mixing approaches at random makes your feed feel unplanned. Consistency is also what grows a local following over time, which we cover in <a href="/blog/grow-instagram-local-business">how a local business grows on Instagram without paid ads</a>.</p>

<p>A few more social tips:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Put text on images in one language,</strong> or keep it very short in both. Two full paragraphs on a graphic are hard to read on a phone.</li>
  <li><strong>Add captions to videos.</strong> Many people watch without sound. Captions in the language that's spoken, plus a short line in the other in your post text, help both groups.</li>
  <li><strong>Reply in the language people write in,</strong> as long as someone on your team can do it well.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you'd like help planning bilingual content that stays on brand, that's part of our <a href="/social-media-marketing">social media and content marketing</a> work.</p>

<h2>Should your website be in Spanish too?</h2>

<p>If a real share of your customers prefer Spanish, a Spanish version of your key pages is worth it. Start small with your home page, your main services, hours and contact. Then add more as you see people use it.</p>

<p>How you set it up matters for search. Google recommends giving each language version its own URL and making it easy for visitors to switch, rather than swapping the language automatically. You can read the details in Google's guide to <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/specialty/international/managing-multi-regional-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">managing multilingual sites</a>. In plain terms:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Give each language its own page, for example <code>/services</code> and <code>/es/servicios</code>.</li>
  <li>Add a clear "English / Español" link on every page so visitors can switch themselves.</li>
  <li>Don't force a language on people based on their browser settings. Let them choose.</li>
  <li>Translate the important stuff fully: headings, buttons, forms and error messages, not just the paragraphs.</li>
</ul>

<p>The writing itself should follow the same rules as your English copy. It should answer real questions in the words people actually search. Our post on <a href="/blog/website-copy-that-ranks-and-converts">website copy that ranks and converts</a> walks through that approach, and it works the same way in Spanish.</p>

<h2>Write it, don't just translate it</h2>

<p>Translation tools are a fine starting point, but they shouldn't be the last step. They often miss tone, pick formal words where a friendly one fits better, and stumble over slogans and wordplay that don't carry across languages.</p>

<p>A better process looks like this:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Start with the message, not the sentence.</strong> What should the customer understand and do? Write that down in a line or two.</li>
  <li><strong>Draft in Spanish with that goal in mind,</strong> or translate and then rewrite freely.</li>
  <li><strong>Have a fluent reviewer from your community read it.</strong> Ideally someone who knows your customers and how they talk. Spanish in New Mexico has its own long-rooted words and phrases, and your customers will notice when the wording feels like it came from somewhere else.</li>
  <li><strong>Read it out loud.</strong> If it sounds stiff in your reviewer's mouth, it'll read stiff on a flyer too.</li>
</ol>

<p>Your slogan or tagline may not translate at all. That's fine. Sometimes the best move is to keep it in its original language as part of your brand and write a separate Spanish line that carries the same feeling.</p>

<h2>Keep your brand consistent in both languages</h2>

<p>Bilingual marketing should still look like one business. Use the same colors, fonts, logo and photo style in both languages. Decide on a few simple rules and write them down, ideally in your <a href="/blog/brand-style-guide-small-business">brand style guide</a>:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Which language comes first on signs and posts.</li>
  <li>How you style the second language (same size, or slightly smaller in a lighter weight).</li>
  <li>The Spanish versions of your key phrases, so everyone uses the same wording every time.</li>
  <li>Who reviews Spanish copy before it goes out.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last one matters most. A shared list of approved phrases saves time and keeps your voice steady no matter who's posting that day.</p>

<h2>Common bilingual marketing mistakes to avoid</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Promising Spanish you can't deliver.</strong> If your ads are in Spanish but nobody can take a call in Spanish, customers feel misled. Be clear about what you offer, like "message us in Spanish and we'll reply by email."</li>
  <li><strong>Publishing machine translation unchecked.</strong> One awkward line on a big sign can stick around for years.</li>
  <li><strong>Making Spanish the small print.</strong> Tiny, faded Spanish under bold English sends a message, even if you didn't mean it.</li>
  <li><strong>Translating once and forgetting.</strong> When your hours, prices or services change, update both languages at the same time.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaning on stereotypes.</strong> Bilingual marketing is about speaking to people clearly, not dressing your brand in clichés. Use real photos of your real business and community instead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A simple way to get started this month</h2>

<p>If this feels like a lot, pick three things and do them well:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Update your Google Business Profile description and post one update in both languages.</li>
  <li>Add Spanish to your most important sign, menu or flyer.</li>
  <li>Choose your social media approach (combined or separate posts), write down the rule, and follow it for a month.</li>
</ol>

<p>Then look at what people respond to. Comments, messages, calls and questions at the counter will tell you where Spanish is helping and where to go next.</p>
